(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test01_2B"] = [{"id":"479c7e82","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Although critics believed that customers would never agree to pay to pick their own produce on farms, such concerns didn’t ______ Booker T. Whatley’s efforts to promote the practice. Thanks in part to Whatley’s determined advocacy, farms that allow visitors to pick their own apples, pumpkins, and other produce can be found throughout the United States.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Although critics believed that customers would never agree to pay to pick their own produce on farms, such concerns didn’t ______ Booker T.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Whatley’s efforts to promote the practice. Thanks in part to Whatley’s determined advocacy, farms that allow visitors to pick their own apples,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pumpkins, and other produce can be found throughout the United States.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["enhance","hinder","misrepresent","aggravate"]},{"id":"710799ae","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Cuttlefish appear to be surprisingly ______ at exercising self-control: in a 2021 study conducted by behavioral ecologist Alexandra Schnell, these cephalopods routinely demonstrated restraint by delaying gratification, waiting for a favorite treat instead of instantly devouring a readily available meal.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Cuttlefish appear to be surprisingly ______ at exercising self-control: in a 2021 study conducted by behavioral ecologist Alexandra Schnell, these","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cephalopods routinely demonstrated restraint by delaying gratification, waiting for a favorite treat instead of instantly devouring a readily","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"available meal.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["awkward","imaginative","manageable","competent"]},{"id":"fcc328c6","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Streams and rivers carry soil and rocks from one location to another. But there is another way for these geological materials to move. Scientists call this process “aeolian transport.” In aeolian transport, winds move small particles of soil or rock over potentially great distances. Geologist Melisa Diaz and her team studied dust in Antarctica to find out if it was moved by aeolian transport. They discovered that the dust matched geological material in Australia. Aeolian transport had carried it from one continent to another, across thousands of miles of open ocean.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Streams and rivers carry soil and rocks from one location to another. But there is another way for these geological materials to move. Scientists","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"call this process “aeolian transport.” In aeolian transport, winds move small particles of soil or rock over potentially great distances. Geologist","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Melisa Diaz and her team studied dust in Antarctica to find out if it was moved by aeolian transport. They discovered that the dust matched","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"geological material in Australia. Aeolian transport had carried it from one continent to another, across thousands of miles of open ocean.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents Melisa Diaz’s remarks about difficulties that her team encountered.","It introduces a scientific term that is used in the discussion that follows.","It emphasizes the surprising nature of the findings that are presented.","It explains the difference between two kinds of geological material."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Scientists","call this process “aeolian transport.”"]},{"id":"e41dfaab","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"In 1929 the Atlantic Monthly published several articles based on newly discovered letters allegedly exchanged between President Abraham Lincoln and a woman named Ann Rutledge. Historians were unable to ______ the authenticity of the letters, however, and quickly dismissed them as a hoax.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1929 the Atlantic Monthly published several articles based on newly discovered letters allegedly exchanged between President Abraham","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Lincoln and a woman named Ann Rutledge. Historians were unable to ______ the authenticity of the letters, however, and quickly dismissed them","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"as a hoax.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["validate","interpret","relate","accommodate"]},{"id":"ce8c03a8","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The Illustrated History of San Mateo County, published in 1878, features several illustrations by Grafton Tyler Brown showing the California county’s farms, residences, and businesses. It was ______ for a business to be featured, since being included suggested that a business was an important part of the community.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Illustrated History of San Mateo County, published in 1878, features several illustrations by Grafton Tyler Brown showing the California","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"county’s farms, residences, and businesses. It was ______ for a business to be featured, since being included suggested that a business was an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"important part of the community.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["risky","misleading","advantageous","unremarkable"]},{"id":"01de46bb","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The Menominee and Anishinaabe peoples have been growing wild rice — known as manoomin in the Ojibwe language—in the Great Lakes region of North America for centuries, but climatic changes are causing lakes to get deeper, thereby threatening wild rice. These plants are extremely ______ to water depth during the “floating leaf” stage of development, and if the water is too deep, the buoyancy of the young wild-rice plants can literally uproot them from the lake bottom, destroying them.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Menominee and Anishinaabe peoples have been growing wild rice—known as manoomin in the Ojibwe language—in the Great Lakes region","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of North America for centuries, but climatic changes are causing lakes to get deeper, thereby threatening wild rice. These plants are extremely","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"______ to water depth during the “floating leaf” stage of development, and if the water is too deep, the buoyancy of the young wild-rice plants can","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"literally uproot them from the lake bottom, destroying them.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["immune","sensitive","limited","receptive"]},{"id":"5fe53347","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Botanist Al Kovaleski has pointed out that maple trees already thrive in a wide variety of climates and thus may ______ changes in climate better than some other tree species do. The alterations maples may undergo in response to a changing climate are likely to be relatively small and easily achieved.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Botanist Al Kovaleski has pointed out that maple trees already thrive in a wide variety of climates and thus may ______ changes in climate better","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"than some other tree species do. The alterations maples may undergo in response to a changing climate are likely to be relatively small and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"easily achieved.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["relocate from","refer to","originate from","adapt to"]},{"id":"b44066dc","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Economist Jingting Fan argues that the effects of international trade may display spatial variation at sub-national levels. For instance, imported goods may reduce expenses for a country’s average consumer, but for consumers living far from ports, high intranational transport costs could ______ the price advantages associated with imports.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Economist Jingting Fan argues that the effects of international trade may display spatial variation at sub-national levels. For instance, imported","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"goods may reduce expenses for a country’s average consumer, but for consumers living far from ports, high intranational transport costs could","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"______ the price advantages associated with imports.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["nullify","denigrate","underestimate","misconstrue"]},{"id":"b1fab3e1","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Violins made by Antonio Stradivari and other craftspeople in the sixteenth to eighteenth centuries in Cremona, Italy, produce a sound that is considered superior to that of modern stringed instruments. Some experts have claimed that the type of wood used to create Cremonese violins is responsible for their prized sound, but modern and Cremonese violins are made of the same kinds of wood: maple and spruce. New analysis, however, has revealed unique indications that the wood in the older violins was chemically treated by the makers, leading researchers to suggest that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Violins made by Antonio Stradivari and other craftspeople in the sixteenth to eighteenth centuries in Cremona, Italy, produce a sound that is","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"considered superior to that of modern stringed instruments. Some experts have claimed that the type of wood used to create Cremonese violins","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"is responsible for their prized sound, but modern and Cremonese violins are made of the same kinds of wood: maple and spruce. New analysis,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"however, has revealed unique indications that the wood in the older violins was chemically treated by the makers, leading researchers to suggest","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["Cremonese violins probably were not considered superior to other instruments at the time they were made.","the sound quality of Cremonese violins results in part from a method the craftspeople used to alter the wood.","if modern violins were made of a wood other than maple or spruce, they likely would sound as good as Cremonese violins.","the current process of making violins is the same process that was used centuries ago by Cremonese craftspeople."]},{"id":"f2b3b53b","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"External shopping cues are a type of marketing that uses obvious messaging — a display featuring a new product, for example, or a “buy one, get one free” offer — to entice consumers to make spontaneous purchases. In a study, data scientist Sam K. Hui and colleagues found that this effect can also be achieved with a less obvious cue: rearranging a store’s layout.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"External shopping cues are a type of marketing that uses obvious messaging—a display featuring a new product, for example, or a “buy one, get","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"one free” offer—to entice consumers to make spontaneous purchases. In a study, data scientist Sam K. Hui and colleagues found that this effect","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"can also be achieved with a less obvious cue: rearranging a store’s layout. The researchers explain that trying to find items in new locations","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"causes shoppers to move through more of the store, exposing them to more products and increasing the likelihood that they’ll buy an item they","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"hadn’t planned on purchasing.","indent":0}],"question":"Which response from a survey given to shoppers who made a purchase at a retail store best supports the researchers’ explanation?","choices":["“I needed to buy some cleaning supplies, but they weren’t in their regular place. While I was looking for them, I saw this interesting notebook and decided to buy it, too.”","“I didn’t buy everything on my shopping list today. I couldn’t find a couple of the items in the store, even though I looked all over for them.”","“The store sent me a coupon for a new brand of soup, so I came here to find out what kinds of soup that brand offers. I decided to buy a few cans because I had the coupon.”","“This store is larger than one that’s closer to where I live, and it carries more products. I came here to buy some things that the other store doesn’t always have.”"]},{"id":"4d38c2ea","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Gorgets, or necklaces with large pendants, have been part of the ceremonial attire of tribes from the US Southeast for centuries. One of the oldest examples, the Fairfield Gorget, was found in Fairfield, Missouri, in 1958. Its overall design resembles that of other art from the region during the Mississippian period (900 – 1600 CE). Yet the image on the gorget is of a jaguar — a species whose range doesn’t extend to Missouri. Jaguar images are common in ancient Mexican art, and Mexico lies squarely in the species range. Therefore, some scholars argue that long- distance trade in Mexican art objects brought the imagery to Missouri, where a local artist could have adopted it and incorporated it into the Fairfield Gorget.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Gorgets, or necklaces with large pendants, have been part of the ceremonial attire of tribes from the US Southeast for centuries. One of the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"oldest examples, the Fairfield Gorget, was found in Fairfield, Missouri, in 1958. Its overall design resembles that of other art from the region","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"during the Mississippian period (900–1600 CE). Yet the image on the gorget is of a jaguar—a species whose range doesn’t extend to Missouri.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Jaguar images are common in ancient Mexican art, and Mexico lies squarely in the species’ range. Therefore, some scholars argue that long-","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"distance trade in Mexican art objects brought the imagery to Missouri, where a local artist could have adopted it and incorporated it into the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Fairfield Gorget.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most strongly support the underlined explanation?","choices":["The Fairfield Gorget is dated not to the Mississippian period but instead to the earlier Woodland period, which ended around 900 CE.","The range of the jaguar is shown to have expanded dramatically after the Mississippian period came to a close around 1600 CE.","An ancient Mexican art object is found at a site that dates to the Mississippian period and is close to where the Fairfield Gorget was found.","Certain works by present-day artists from Southeastern tribes reflect outside cultural influences, including contemporary Mexican art."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["long-","distance trade in Mexican art objects brought the imagery to Missouri, where a local artist could have adopted it and incorporated it into the","Fairfield Gorget."]},{"id":"6e0e0de1","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Aerogels are highly porous foams consisting mainly of tiny air pockets within a solidified gel. These lightweight materials are often applied to spacecraft and other equipment required to withstand extreme conditions, as they provide excellent insulation despite typically being brittle and eventually fracturing due to degradation from repeated exposure to high heat. Now, Xiangfeng Duan of the University of California, Los Angeles, and colleagues have developed an aerogel with uniquely flexible properties. Unlike earlier aerogels, Duan’s team’s material contracts rather than expands when heated and fully recovers after compressing to just 5% of its original volume, suggesting that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Aerogels are highly porous foams consisting mainly of tiny air pockets within a solidified gel. These lightweight materials are often applied to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"spacecraft and other equipment required to withstand extreme conditions, as they provide excellent insulation despite typically being brittle and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"eventually fracturing due to degradation from repeated exposure to high heat. Now, Xiangfeng Duan of the University of California, Los Angeles,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and colleagues have developed an aerogel with uniquely flexible properties. Unlike earlier aerogels, Duan’s team’s material contracts rather than","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"expands when heated and fully recovers after compressing to just 5% of its original volume, suggesting that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["the aerogel’s remarkable flexibility results from its higher proportion of air pockets to solidified gel as compared to other aerogels.","the aerogel’s overall strength is greater than that of other insulators but its ability to withstand exposure to intense heat is lower.","the aerogel will be more effective as an insulator for uses that involve gradual temperature shifts than for those that involve rapid heat increases.","the aerogel will be less prone to the structural weakness that ultimately causes most other aerogels to break down with use."]},{"id":"47f2cddd","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"“The Rock and the Sea” is an 1893 poem by Charlotte Perkins Gilman. In the poem, a rock is portrayed as intending to confront and restrain the sea: ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"“The Rock and the Sea” is an 1893 poem by Charlotte Perkins Gilman. In the poem, a rock is portrayed as intending to confront and restrain the","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"sea: ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which quotation from “The Rock and the Sea” most effectively illustrates the claim?","choices":["“I am the Rock. Black midnight falls; / The terrible breakers rise like walls; / With curling lips and gleaming teeth / They plunge and tear at my bones beneath.”","“I am the Sea. I hold the land / As one holds an apple in his hand, / Hold it fast with sleepless eyes, / Watching the continents sink and rise.”","“I am the Rock, presumptuous Sea! / I am set to encounter thee. / Angry and loud or gentle and still, / I am set here to limit thy power, and I will!”","“I am the Sea. The earth I sway; / Granite to me is potter’s clay; / Under the touch of my careless waves / It rises in turrets and sinks in caves.”"]},{"id":"92c2564d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Utah is home to Pando, a colony of about 47,000 quaking aspen trees that all share a single root system. Pando is one of the largest single organisms by mass on Earth, but ecologists are worried that its growth is declining in part because of grazing by animals. The ecologists say that strong fences could prevent deer from eating young trees and help Pando start thriving again.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Utah is home to Pando, a colony of about 47,000 quaking aspen trees that all share a single root system. Pando is one of the largest single","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"organisms by mass on Earth, but ecologists are worried that its growth is declining in part because of grazing by animals. The ecologists say","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that strong fences could prevent deer from eating young trees and help Pando start thriving again.","indent":0}],"question":"According to the text, why are ecologists worried about Pando?","choices":["It isn’t growing at the same rate it used to.","It isn’t producing young trees anymore.","It can’t grow into new areas because it is blocked by fences.","Its root system can’t support many more new trees."]},{"id":"086dd8cc","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"The morphological novelty of echinoderms — marine invertebrates with radial symmetry, usually starlike, around a central point — impedes comparisons with most other animals, in which bilateral symmetry on an anterior-posterior (head to tail) axis through a trunk is typical. Particularly puzzling are sea stars, thought to have evolved a headless layout from a known bilateral origin. Applying genomic knowledge of Saccoglossus kowalevskii acorn worms (close relatives of sea stars, and thus expected to have similar markers for corresponding anatomical regions) to the body patterning genes of Patiria miniata sea stars, Laurent Formery et al. observed activity only in anterior genes across P. miniata’s entire body and some posterior genes limited to the edges, suggesting that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The morphological novelty of echinoderms—marine invertebrates with radial symmetry, usually starlike, around a central point—impedes","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"comparisons with most other animals, in which bilateral symmetry on an anterior-posterior (head to tail) axis through a trunk is typical.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Particularly puzzling are sea stars, thought to have evolved a headless layout from a known bilateral origin. Applying genomic knowledge of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Saccoglossus kowalevskii acorn worms (close relatives of sea stars, and thus expected to have similar markers for corresponding anatomical","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"regions) to the body patterning genes of Patiria miniata sea stars, Laurent Formery et al. observed activity only in anterior genes across P.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"miniata’s entire body and some posterior genes limited to the edges, suggesting that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["despite the greater prevalence of anterior genes in sea stars genetic makeup, posterior genes active at the body’s perimeter are primarily responsible for the starlike layout that distinguishes sea stars radial symmetry from that of other echinoderms.","contrary to the belief that they evolved from early ancestors with the bilateral form typical of many other animals, sea stars instead originated with an atypical body layout that was neither bilaterally nor radially symmetrical.","although the two species are closely related, there is only minimal correspondence in the genetic markers for head, tail, and trunk region development in P. miniata sea stars and S. kowalevskii acorn worms.","rather than undergoing changes resulting in the eventual elimination of a head region in their radial body plan, as previously assumed, sea stars morphology evolved to completely lack a trunk and consist primarily of a head region."]},{"id":"9994ae0d","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Artist Yto Barrada’s exhibition Ways to Baffle the Wind incorporates sculptures, textiles, and films. Barrada’s pieces, utilizing elements as disparate as plant-dyed fabrics, wire crab traps filled with stones, and cotton balls dangling above a fan, ______ the ways humans attempt to organize and regulate nature.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Artist Yto Barrada’s exhibition Ways to Baffle the Wind incorporates sculptures, textiles, and films. Barrada’s pieces, utilizing elements as","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"disparate as plant-dyed fabrics, wire crab traps filled with stones, and cotton balls dangling above a fan, ______ the ways humans attempt to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"organize and regulate nature.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["explore","has explored","explores","exploring"]},{"id":"b74f676f","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Classical composer Florence Price’s 1927 move to Chicago marked a turning point in her career. It was there that Price premiered her First Symphony — a piece that was praised for blending traditional Romantic motifs with aspects of Black folk music —and ______ supportive relationships with other Black artists.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Classical composer Florence Price’s 1927 move to Chicago marked a turning point in her career. It was there that Price premiered her First","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Symphony—a piece that was praised for blending traditional Romantic motifs with aspects of Black folk music—and ______ supportive","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"relationships with other Black artists.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["developing","developed","to develop","having developed"]},{"id":"e2759b92","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Occupying a significant part of modern-day Nigeria, the Kingdom of Benin was one of the major powers in West Africa between the thirteenth and nineteenth centuries. It ______ ruled by Oba Ewuare I from 1440 to 1473.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Occupying a significant part of modern-day Nigeria, the Kingdom of Benin was one of the major powers in West Africa between the thirteenth","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and nineteenth centuries. It ______ ruled by Oba Ewuare I from 1440 to 1473.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["is","will be","has been","was"]},{"id":"a03008de","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"The Proto-Nilotic language, common ancestor of fifty-five African languages with similar linguistic properties, ______ like all protolanguages, hypothetical: there’s no direct evidence these ancestral languages actually existed.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Proto-Nilotic language, common ancestor of fifty-five African languages with similar linguistic properties, ______ like all protolanguages,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"hypothetical: there’s no direct evidence these ancestral languages actually existed.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["is,","are,","have been,","were,"]},{"id":"6d247c13","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"The Boston Saloon was one of the most popular African American – owned establishments in nineteenth-century Nevada. ______ by businessman William A.G. Brown, the saloon was known to offer elegant accommodations and an inclusive environment.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Boston Saloon was one of the most popular African American–owned establishments in nineteenth-century Nevada. ______ by businessman","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"William A.G. Brown, the saloon was known to offer elegant accommodations and an inclusive environment.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Created","Creates","Creating","Create"]},{"id":"59209b6d","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Based on genetic evidence, archaeologists have generally agreed that reindeer domestication began in the eleventh century CE. However, since uncovering fragments of a 2,000-year-old reindeer training harness in northern Siberia, ______ may have begun much earlier.","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["researcher Robert Losey has argued that domestication","researcher Robert Losey’s argument is that domestication","domestication, researcher Robert Losey has argued,","the argument researcher Robert Losey has made is that domestication"]},{"id":"79c6a01e","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: The engineer Robert Fulton designed the Clermont steamboat in 1807. He designed it in New York City. Clermont was the world’s first commercially successful steamboat. The city of Fulton, Missouri, is named after Robert Fulton. New York City’s Fulton Street is named after him. The student wants to indicate how Fulton, Missouri, got its name.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The engineer Robert Fulton designed the Clermont steamboat in 1807.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"He designed it in New York City.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Clermont was the world’s first commercially successful steamboat.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The city of Fulton, Missouri, is named after Robert Fulton.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"New York City’s Fulton Street is named after him.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Fulton, Missouri, shares its name with Fulton Street in New York City.","Fulton Street is in New York City, where the steamboat Clermont was designed in 1807.","Designed in 1807 in New York City, Clermont was the first commercially successful steamboat.","Fulton, Missouri, is named after Robert Fulton, designer of the first commercially successful steamboat."]},{"id":"17e49403","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"When, in the 1800s, geologists first realized that much of Earth had once been covered by great sheets of ice, some theorized that the phenomenon was cyclical, occurring at regular intervals. Each Ice Age is so destructive, though, that it largely erases the geological evidence of its predecessor. ______ geologists were unable to confirm the theory of cyclical Ice Ages until the 1960s.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"When, in the 1800s, geologists first realized that much of Earth had once been covered by great sheets of ice, some theorized that the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"phenomenon was cyclical, occurring at regular intervals. Each Ice Age is so destructive, though, that it largely erases the geological evidence of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"its predecessor. ______ geologists were unable to confirm the theory of cyclical Ice Ages until the 1960s.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Hence,","Moreover,","Nevertheless,","Next,"]},{"id":"20733eac","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"It has long been thought that humans first crossed a land bridge into the Americas approximately 13,000 years ago. ______ based on radiocarbon dating of samples uncovered in Mexico, a research team recently suggested that humans may have arrived more than 30,000 years ago — much earlier than previously thought.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"It has long been thought that humans first crossed a land bridge into the Americas approximately 13,000 years ago. ______ based on radiocarbon","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"dating of samples uncovered in Mexico, a research team recently suggested that humans may have arrived more than 30,000 years ago—much","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"earlier than previously thought.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["As a result,","Similarly,","However,","In conclusion,"]},{"id":"4b376902","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: NASA uses rovers, large remote vehicles with wheels, to explore the surface of Mars. NASA’s rovers can’t explore regions inaccessible to wheeled vehicles. Rovers are also heavy, making them difficult to land on the planet’s surface. Microprobes, robotic probes that weigh as little as 50 milligrams, could be deployed virtually anywhere on the surface of Mars. Microprobes have been proposed as an alternative to rovers. The student wants to explain an advantage of microprobes.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"NASA uses rovers, large remote vehicles with wheels, to explore the surface of Mars.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"NASA’s rovers can’t explore regions inaccessible to wheeled vehicles.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Rovers are also heavy, making them difficult to land on the planet’s surface.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Microprobes, robotic probes that weigh as little as 50 milligrams, could be deployed virtually anywhere on the surface of Mars.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Microprobes have been proposed as an alternative to rovers.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Despite being heavy, NASA’s rovers can land successfully on the surface of Mars.","Microprobes, which weigh as little as 50 milligrams, could explore areas of Mars that are inaccessible to NASA’s heavy, wheeled rovers.","NASA currently uses its rovers on Mars, but microprobes have been proposed as an alternative.","Though they are different sizes, both microprobes and rovers can be used to explore the surface of Mars."]},{"id":"a4366255","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Musicians around the world have used protest songs to raise awareness about human rights violations. US folk singer Aunt Molly Jackson released the protest song “Poor Miner’s Farewell” in 1932. It exposed the unlivable wages and dangerous working conditions coal miners faced in Kentucky during the 1920s and 1930s. South African singer-songwriter Hugh Masekela released the protest song “Bring Him Back Home” in 1987. It called on the South African government to free Nelson Mandela, an anti-apartheid leader who’d been unjustly imprisoned. The student wants to contrast the song “Poor Miner’s Farewell” with the song “Bring Him Back Home.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Musicians around the world have used protest songs to raise awareness about human rights violations.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"US folk singer Aunt Molly Jackson released the protest song “Poor Miner’s Farewell” in 1932.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"It exposed the unlivable wages and dangerous working conditions coal miners faced in Kentucky during the 1920s and 1930s.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"South African singer-songwriter Hugh Masekela released the protest song “Bring Him Back Home” in 1987.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"It called on the South African government to free Nelson Mandela, an anti-apartheid leader who’d been unjustly imprisoned.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The songs “Poor Miner’s Farewell” and “Bring Him Back Home” both raised awareness about human rights violations.","While both are protest songs, “Poor Miner’s Farewell” is about coal miners in Kentucky, whereas “Bring Him Back Home” is about the anti- apartheid leader Nelson Mandela.","Hugh Masekela’s song “Bring Him Back Home,” released in 1987, called on the South African government to free Nelson Mandela.","Released in 1932 by Aunt Molly Jackson, the song “Poor Miner’s Farewell” was a protest against the unlivable wages and dangerous working conditions faced by Kentucky coal miners."]},{"id":"92dec236","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Maika’i Tubbs is a Native Hawaiian sculptor and installation artist. His work has been shown in the United States, Canada, Japan, and Germany, among other places. Many of his sculptures feature discarded objects. His work Erasure (2008) includes discarded audiocassette tapes and magnets. His work Home Grown (2009) includes discarded pushpins, plastic plates and forks, and wood. The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two works.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Maika’i Tubbs is a Native Hawaiian sculptor and installation artist.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"His work has been shown in the United States, Canada, Japan, and Germany, among other places.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Many of his sculptures feature discarded objects.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"His work Erasure (2008) includes discarded audiocassette tapes and magnets.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"His work Home Grown (2009) includes discarded pushpins, plastic plates and forks, and wood.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Erasure (2008) uses discarded objects such as audiocassette tapes and magnets; Home Grown (2009), however, includes pushpins, plastic plates and forks, and wood.","Tubbs’s work, which often features discarded objects, has been shown both within the United States and abroad.","Like many of Tubbs’s sculptures, both Erasure and Home Grown include discarded objects: Erasure uses audiocassette tapes, and Home Grown uses plastic forks.","Tubbs completed Erasure in 2008 and Home Grown in 2009."]}];
